WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.640
We are buying a company.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.250
What else are we buying?

00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:07.745
Five questions decide whether the
deal brings value or an incident.

00:00:09.083 --> 00:00:11.273
First, is an attacker already inside?

00:00:11.713 --> 00:00:13.843
Marriott bought Starwood in 2016.

00:00:14.268 --> 00:00:15.958
According to the UK ICO,

00:00:16.528 --> 00:00:19.838
an attacker got into
Starwood's systems in 2014.

00:00:20.238 --> 00:00:22.628
Marriott found it in September 2018.

00:00:23.073 --> 00:00:28.753
The ICO fined Marriott £18.4 million
for not securing the systems

00:00:28.753 --> 00:00:29.723
it had acquired.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:32.753
Second, what are we really buying?

00:00:33.068 --> 00:00:36.358
The target's asset list
shows what its IT team runs.

00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:38.783
It rarely shows the agency website,

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:42.213
the cloud account a developer
opened on a company card,

00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:45.423
or the remote access box nobody patched.

00:00:45.843 --> 00:00:48.173
What nobody listed, nobody patches.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.760
Third, when do we connect?

00:00:51.185 --> 00:00:54.815
Linking networks on day one
links their weaknesses to ours.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.380
We suggest a bridge period,
with controlled gateways.

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:01.155
Connect only after the
compromise assessment,

00:01:01.685 --> 00:01:04.275
the admin password resets and MFA.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.700
Fourth, who pays for a breach
that started before closing?

00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:12.565
A warranty of no known breach
means little if nobody looked.

00:01:12.855 --> 00:01:15.125
Consider a specific cyber indemnity.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.980
W&amp;I insurance typically excludes
what was already known at signing.

00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:23.773
Fifth, which personal
data comes with the deal?

00:01:24.003 --> 00:01:27.953
Customer and staff records come
with the promises made about them.

00:01:28.158 --> 00:01:31.708
An old breach found after
closing can still need reporting.

00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:34.638
Under GDPR Article 33,

00:01:35.048 --> 00:01:38.138
the clock is 72 hours from becoming aware.

00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:40.393
What good looks like.

00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:43.668
A compromise assessment
before we connect anything.

00:01:44.028 --> 00:01:46.918
A full inventory with an
owner for each system.

00:01:47.178 --> 00:01:50.548
NIS2 Article 21 lists asset management.

00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:51.918
A staged connection.

00:01:52.523 --> 00:01:55.893
A cyber indemnity,
and the insurer's answer in writing.

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:58.998
A data inventory before any systems merge.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.140
A suggested first step.

00:02:02.425 --> 00:02:04.575
Ask who decides when the networks connect.

00:02:05.015 --> 00:02:06.145
And on what conditions.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.990
Ask for a compromise assessment first.

00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:13.033
In a deal, security is part of the price.

00:02:13.393 --> 00:02:14.643
Look before you connect.

